Hi Maureen,
I’m not sure I have any great solutions for you but one thing to keep in mind is that interpreting the % change in balance can be tricky if the initial balance was quite good.  e.g., there may be an apparently large reduction in balance when looking at the % change, when really it’s just that the amount of imbalance before the matching was tiny.  So I would encourage you to focus more on the balance measures after the matching rather than the % change.   

If you still see some large imbalances you may have to change the approach or investigate whether there are some parts of the treatment group that just don’t have good comparison group matches.

Liz




